docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:699:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_012upnhK2tNXzAu9SBNGj8hU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DCtzscciqRdZEYJ99VWxQd","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T16.md","content":"# T16 — Phase 2 — Event log compaction snapshot + bootstrap-from-snapshot\n\n**Phase:** 2\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** T12, T13, T14\n**Spec anchor:** [§Storage Design](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules.\n\n## Goal\n\nThe spec requires append-only event storage with compaction: at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp. This task implements that snapshot writer, the bootstrap-from-snapshot loader that lets the daemon start without replaying full history, the daemon-managed background scheduler that runs compaction off the hot path, and the architecture doc that records the format version, schedule semantics, and recovery procedure. Snapshots are the only mechanism preventing unbounded event-log growth for long-running repos; without this task, the event log substrate is incomplete.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Event log compaction: append-only storage will grow unboundedly for long-running repos. The storage design must include a compaction snapshot strategy: at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp.\"\n\n> \"The snapshot format must be versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history. Compaction must be a daemon-managed background operation, not a user-initiated manual step.\"\n\n> \"Every background consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, and observable.\"\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs:\n  - T12 — append-only `events` table; the compactor must respect the UPDATE/DELETE trigger and use a sanctioned \"truncate up to snapshot timestamp\" path (move or archive rows, then DELETE under an `ALLOW` flag set only by the compactor; document the mechanism).\n  - T13 — `EventWriter` for emitting `MemoryConsolidated` or a dedicated \"snapshot taken\" event into the log itself.\n  - T14 — `EventReader` for the bootstrap loader (replays only events after the snapshot timestamp).\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/storage/` — existing patterns for opening side files in `.lattice/`.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/graph/` — current graph snapshot serialization (if any) to model after.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/` — memory state shape that snapshots must serialize.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/src/server.rs` (or equivalent) — daemon lifecycle into which the background scheduler will hook.\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard §Error handling, §No broken windows, §Observability; Lattice rule that operational behavior is documented in Markdown with heading references from code.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. In `events/snapshot.rs`, define `pub struct Snapshot { format_version: u16, taken_at: DateTime<Utc>, up_to_event_id: EventId, graph_state: GraphSnapshot, memory_state: MemorySnapshot, content_hash: SnapshotHash }`. `format_version` is a `u16` starting at 1 and is the only field whose meaning must never change — bootstrap loaders dispatch on it.\n2. Define the snapshot file layout (`.lattice/snapshots/snapshot-<event_id>-<unix_micros>.bin`): a fixed 64-byte header (`magic[8]`, `format_version`, `flags`, `header_len`, `content_offset`, `content_len`, `content_hash[32]`) followed by a `bincode`- or `serde_cbor`-encoded body. The header is independently readable without the body decoder so loaders can refuse incompatible versions before allocating.\n3. Implement `Snapshot::write(path, graph, memory, up_to_event_id) -> Result<SnapshotHandle, SnapshotError>` and `Snapshot::read(path) -> Result<Snapshot, SnapshotError>`.\n4. In `events/compaction.rs`, define `pub struct Compactor { store: Arc<EventStore>, writer: Arc<EventWriter>, graph: Arc<GraphHandle>, memory: Arc<MemoryHandle>, config: CompactionConfig }` where `CompactionConfig` carries `interval: Duration`, `min_events_since_last`, `snapshot_dir: PathBuf`, `retain_snapshots: usize`.\n5. Implement `Compactor::run_once(&self) -> Result<CompactionReport, CompactionError>`:\n   1. Acquire a compaction lock so concurrent compactions are impossible.\n   2. Snapshot current graph + memory state and the `up_to_event_id` cursor (latest `event_id` at snapshot time).\n   3. Write snapshot file atomically (temp-file + rename).\n   4. Verify the snapshot reads back cleanly (round-trip check) before truncating anything.\n   5. Truncate the event log to rows with `event_id > up_to_event_id` using the sanctioned truncate path from T12 — the trigger remains in place for normal writes.\n   6. Emit a `MemoryConsolidated` event (or a dedicated `SnapshotTaken` event added to T11 if the spec list permits — otherwise reuse `MemoryConsolidated` with a tagged summary) so the new log still records that compaction happened.\n   7. Rotate old snapshots beyond `retain_snapshots`.\n   8. Return a `CompactionReport` with bytes written, events truncated, duration, and snapshot path.\n6. Implement `Compactor::spawn_scheduler(self) -> SchedulerHandle` that runs `run_once` on a `tokio::time::interval` (or equivalent) using a dedicated background task — never on the request thread. `SchedulerHandle::shutdown()` lets the daemon stop the loop cleanly.\n7. Implement `Bootstrap::load(snapshot_path: &Path, event_store: &EventStore) -> Result<BootstrappedState, BootstrapError>`: read the snapshot header, dispatch on `format_version`, decode the body, then replay events with `event_id > snapshot.up_to_event_id` via `EventReader::stream`. Fail loudly if any post-snapshot event references state not present in the snapshot (`BootstrapError::ReferenceUnresolved`).\n8. Define typed errors throughout: `SnapshotError { IoFailed, HeaderUnrecognized { magic }, FormatVersionUnsupported { found, max }, ContentHashMismatch, Serde }`; `CompactionError { LockHeld, SnapshotFailed(SnapshotError), TruncateFailed(EventStoreError), VerifyFailed }`; `BootstrapError { SnapshotMissing, FormatVersionUnsupported, ReferenceUnresolved { event_id, missing_ref } }`.\n9. Wire `Compactor::spawn_scheduler` into the daemon lifecycle so it starts on daemon boot and shuts down on signal; this task's wiring is minimal (one call site), with the broader daemon-lifecycle integration covered by existing patterns.\n10. In `events/snapshot_tests.rs`, write tests for:\n    - snapshot write + read round-trip preserves graph and memory state byte-for-byte.\n    - format-version mismatch on read returns `FormatVersionUnsupported` and does not panic.\n    - content-hash mismatch returns `ContentHashMismatch`.\n    - compaction is idempotent if run twice with no new events.\n    - bootstrap from snapshot + tail-events reconstitutes the same state as a full replay.\n    - scheduler shutdown cancels an in-progress compaction cleanly without partial truncation.\n11. Write `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-event-log-compaction.md` with sections: `## Snapshot format`, `## Compaction schedule`, `## Bootstrap procedure`, `## Recovery from corrupt snapshot`, `## Retention policy`, `## Observability`. The doc is the authoritative reference; cite it from `events/snapshot.rs` and `events/compaction.rs` via Markdown heading references in module docs.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/snapshot.rs` — `Snapshot`, header layout, write/read APIs, format-version dispatch.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/compaction.rs` — `Compactor`, `CompactionConfig`, `CompactionReport`, `SchedulerHandle`, `Bootstrap::load`.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/snapshot_tests.rs` — round-trip, format-version, content-hash, idempotence, bootstrap-equivalence, scheduler-shutdown tests.\n- `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-event-log-compaction.md` — versioned snapshot format, schedule semantics, bootstrap procedure, recovery, retention, observability.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/snapshot.rs` — snapshot module exists.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/compaction.rs` — compaction module exists.\n- `test -f docs/architecture/2026-05-16-event-log-compaction.md` — architecture doc exists.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib events::snapshot_tests` — every snapshot/compaction/bootstrap test passes.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file.\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10.\n- Schema parity: snapshot body fields match the graph and memory shapes they capture; format-version bump for any field change.\n- No silent error handling — every `SnapshotError` / `CompactionError` / `BootstrapError` propagated; the scheduler logs at `error` and continues to the next interval rather than panicking.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, commented-out code.\n- For SQLite: the sanctioned truncate path is a documented exception to the append-only trigger; comment in code and in the architecture doc explains why.\n- Use `tracing::info_span!(\"compaction\", interval = ?)`, `tracing::warn!` on retention rotation, `tracing::error!` on `CompactionError`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content.\n- [ ] Every verification command passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: snapshot format, write, read, compactor, scheduler, bootstrap, retention, and architecture doc all in this task. Compaction is daemon-managed (not manual) per the spec.\n- [ ] Architecture doc updated in the same task if behavior is publicly observable — `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-event-log-compaction.md` is created in this task with the mandatory sections listed in Steps §11.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13349,"cache_read_input_tokens":58741,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":13349,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01KTEqX34f6x4nqNqXUnhApL","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"b8f23fff-95da-4cff-903a-c935ac16bcaa","request_id":"req_011Cb7MuQVuoQTj59PzQP5pY","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 2+3+Gate task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:844:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_019ScAFhwj15KgrT4jB8Uvi8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KGGVeep55c7eVz5hDjJSs8","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T82.md","content":"# T82 — Phase 11 — Recovery + corrupted-event handling tests\n\n**Phase:** 11\n**Type:** tests\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** R79\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 11: Hardening](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-11-hardening), [§Storage Design](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design), [§3. Event Log](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#3-event-log), [§Non-Negotiable Product Properties](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#non-negotiable-product-properties)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task delivers two test modules that prove the daemon survives the failure modes Phase 11 names explicitly: recovery from a partial-write or crash, and handling of corrupted event payloads. Spec §Storage Design requires event-log compaction snapshots to be \"versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history\"; spec §3 makes the event log replayable; spec §Phase 11 deliverables list \"recovery tests\" and \"corrupted-event handling\" separately. Without this suite, a single corrupted event payload could brick the daemon and a partial snapshot would silently lose history — both unacceptable per spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties (\"every background consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, and observable\").\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Phase 11 deliverables: ... recovery tests; corrupted-event handling; ...\"\n\n> \"The event log should be append-only. Derived graph and memory state can be rebuilt from source files and events.\" (spec §Storage Design)\n\n> \"Event log compaction: ... at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp. The snapshot format must be versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history.\" (spec §Storage Design)\n\n> \"Tests for ordering, replay, corruption handling, and workspace scoping.\" (spec §Phase 2 deliverables)\n\n> \"Failure paths emit useful events without hiding errors.\" (spec §Phase 2 DoD)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R79 (contract gate) — confirms the daemon API the tests exercise is stable.\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/` (Phase 2 event writer/reader, payload spillover, compaction snapshot routines).\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/` (Phase 3 memory store).\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/storage/` (SQLite connection pool, journal mode).\n- Standards files to honor: `coding.md` (tests as documentation, error handling, observability); `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §Storage Design (event log compaction snapshot, versioned format), §3 Event Log (append-only, replayable), §Phase 2 deliverables (corruption handling), §Phase 11 deliverables (recovery + corrupted-event), and §Non-Negotiable Product Properties so the recovery and corruption invariants are pinned.\n2. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/hardening/recovery_tests.rs` covering:\n   - **Replay from snapshot**: produce an event log, trigger a compaction snapshot at timestamp T, append more events after T, simulate daemon restart, assert the daemon bootstraps from the snapshot + replays events-after-T to reconstruct the exact same graph + memory state.\n   - **Replay from event log only**: delete the snapshot, restart the daemon, assert it can replay the full event log to reconstruct the state (slower path, but must work).\n   - **Partial snapshot recovery**: write a snapshot file that is truncated mid-write (simulate crash during compaction); on restart, assert the daemon detects the truncated snapshot, ignores it, and falls back to full event-log replay without data loss.\n   - **Partial event write recovery**: write an event whose row is committed but whose spillover-payload row failed to commit (simulate crash between two writes); on restart, assert the daemon detects the dangling event id, emits a `recovery` event under spec §Phase 2 DoD \"failure paths emit useful events without hiding errors\", and either reconciles or quarantines the event with an explicit log line.\n   - **WAL recovery**: kill the daemon mid-write (simulate `kill -9`); on restart, assert SQLite WAL recovery completes cleanly and no events are lost.\n3. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/hardening/corruption_tests.rs` covering:\n   - **Corrupted event payload**: write a valid event envelope but a corrupted (random bytes) payload to the spillover table; on read, assert the daemon emits a `corruption` event, returns an explicit error for that event, and continues processing subsequent events without crashing.\n   - **Mismatched payload hash**: write an event whose `payload_hash` does not match the actual payload bytes; on read, assert the daemon detects the hash mismatch, logs a `corruption` event, and quarantines the row.\n   - **Invalid event kind**: write a row whose `kind` column contains a string not in the 20-kind enum; on read, assert the daemon rejects the row with a clear error rather than panicking.\n   - **Invalid stable reference**: write an event with a `StableRef` pointing to a non-existent file/symbol/memory; on read, assert the daemon emits a `dangling-reference` event and surfaces the row in a quarantine query without crashing.\n   - **Truncated event log file**: physically truncate the events SQLite file mid-page; on open, assert the daemon detects the truncation, runs SQLite recovery, and continues with the events it can read while emitting a recovery log line.\n   - **Snapshot version mismatch**: write a snapshot with a higher `format_version` than the daemon supports; on read, assert the daemon refuses to bootstrap and emits a clear \"snapshot format too new\" error rather than silently loading garbage.\n4. Each test asserts the daemon survives (no panic), emits the documented recovery / corruption event under spec §Phase 2 DoD, and that the surviving state remains queryable.\n5. Run `cd daemon && cargo build --release` then `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib hardening::recovery_tests` and `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib hardening::corruption_tests` and confirm every test passes.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/hardening/recovery_tests.rs` — replay-from-snapshot, replay-from-event-log, partial-snapshot, partial-event-write, and WAL recovery scenarios.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/hardening/corruption_tests.rs` — corrupted-payload, mismatched-hash, invalid-kind, invalid-stable-ref, truncated-file, and snapshot-version-mismatch scenarios.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/hardening/recovery_tests.rs` — confirms the recovery test module exists.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/hardening/corruption_tests.rs` — confirms the corruption test module exists.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib hardening::recovery_tests` — runs every recovery test and confirms every invariant assertion passes.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib hardening::corruption_tests` — runs every corruption test and confirms every invariant assertion passes.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file (split per scenario family if it exceeds).\n- Function length ≤50 lines per test; setup factored into helpers (snapshot-builder, payload-corrupter, daemon-restarter).\n- Tests as documentation: each test name describes the failure it covers (e.g. `test_partial_snapshot_falls_back_to_full_replay`, `test_corrupted_payload_quarantines_row_without_panic`).\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- No silent error handling — every assertion failure panics with a message naming the scenario, the expected recovery behavior, and the observed behavior.\n- Observability: tests assert specific `tracing` events were emitted (recovery, corruption, dangling-reference) so production debuggability is validated alongside correctness.\n- Doc citation: comments cite `## Storage Design` (snapshot format), `## 3. Event Log` (replayability), `## Phase 2: Event Log Substrate` (failure paths emit useful events), `## Phase 11: Hardening` (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Markdown Heading References).\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] Every recovery and corruption scenario named above is covered with one test per behavior.\n- [ ] Replay-from-snapshot is exercised end-to-end, supporting the runbook procedure that T86 documents.\n- [ ] No deferred work: every recovery + corruption surface from spec §Storage Design and §3 covered; partial-index (T83), migration (T84), docs (T85), runbook (T86) are sibling Phase 11 tasks, not deferrals.\n- [ ] After build, deploy per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy procedure.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":101320,"cache_read_input_tokens":10845,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":101320,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"62d7b6a9-8914-4237-9396-e7e3f0ffcad0","request_id":"req_011Cb7NGicAyFMeCwEAwKwU2","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/00-decompose.log:895:{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_019ScAFhwj15KgrT4jB8Uvi8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HnSssuUhJHmp9nJUExrmPp","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T86.md","content":"# T86 — Phase 11 — Operator runbook + recovery playbook\n\n**Phase:** 11\n**Type:** docs\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** T82, T85\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 11: Hardening](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-11-hardening), [§Storage Design](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design), [§Documentation Requirements](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#documentation-requirements)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task delivers the two operational documents that turn the architecture suite into something an on-call human can actually use under pressure: the daily-operations runbook and the recovery playbook. The runbook is the day-in-the-life: how to deploy, how to verify health, how to read the metrics, how to respond to common alerts. The recovery playbook is the worst-day-of-the-week document: what to do when the daemon crashes, when the event log is corrupted, when memory is contradictory at scale, when migration breaks. It includes the binding deploy sequence from `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy and the replay-from-snapshot procedure proved viable by T82 recovery tests.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Phase 11 deliverables: ... documentation for setup, operations, memory semantics, and workflow behavior.\"\n\n> \"Event log compaction: ... at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp. The snapshot format must be versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history.\" (spec §Storage Design — drives the replay-from-snapshot procedure)\n\n> \"Required docs: ... operator guide; ...\" (spec §Documentation Requirements — runbook is the deep operator-action surface)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: T82 (recovery + corruption tests — provides the proven procedures), T85 (documentation suite — provides the cross-link anchors for memory/event/consolidation/retrieval/verification docs).\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy — the canonical deploy sequence quoted in this runbook.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/hardening/recovery_tests.rs` (from T82) — the test that exercises the replay-from-snapshot procedure this playbook documents.\n  - `docs/operator-guide/2026-05-16-operator-guide.md` (from T85) — the runbook cross-references its sections.\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-event-log-design.md` (from T85) — the playbook cross-references the compaction-snapshot semantics.\n- Standards files to honor: `coding.md`; `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy and Markdown heading references.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read T85's operator guide and event-log design doc so the runbook layers on top without duplicating their content.\n2. Create `docs/operator-guide/2026-05-16-runbook.md`. Required headings:\n   - `## Daily operations` — required by verification. Cover: starting/stopping the daemon, checking health via the review panel from T76 (`consolidationQueueView`, `indexingHealthView`, `workspaceGraphHealthView`), reading the metrics report from T68/T69 (Phase 9), reviewing accepted/rejected promotions weekly.\n   - `## Deploy sequence` — quote the canonical sequence from `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy verbatim: `pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/`. Document the `sleep 2` between `pkill` and `cp` per the canonical sequence. Document the verification steps: confirm the new binary is running via `pgrep -fa lattice`; confirm the extension picks up the new binary via the activity bar.\n   - `## Health checks` — list the green/yellow/red thresholds for each indicator in the indexing health and graph health views: parser failure rate, vector index freshness, FTS row count, event log ingestion rate, compaction snapshot age. Each threshold cites the spec heading that motivates it.\n   - `## Alerts and response` — for each alert (queue depth at bound, dropped consolidation jobs, broken references, stale-edge spike, hot-path P99 budget breach), document the on-call response: which review-panel view to open, which log target to inspect, what to escalate.\n   - `## Memory hygiene` — operator weekly tasks: review promotion queue, review contradiction queue, audit stale memory, audit usefulness metrics from T68/T69.\n   - `## Backups` — document the backup strategy for `.lattice/` (graph.db, memories.db, events.db, snapshots, vector index) and the restore procedure (cross-link to the recovery playbook).\n3. Create `docs/operator-guide/2026-05-16-recovery-playbook.md`. Required headings:\n   - `## Recovery procedures` — required by verification. Cover the full recovery decision tree: daemon won't start, daemon starts but indexing fails, daemon indexing succeeds but queries return empty, queries return stale data, memory state is contradictory, snapshot file is missing or corrupted.\n   - `## Replay from snapshot` — required by verification. The binding procedure proved viable by T82 recovery tests. Steps: (1) stop the daemon (`pkill -f lattice`); (2) verify the snapshot file exists at `.lattice/snapshots/<latest>.snap`; (3) verify the snapshot file integrity (file size, format version header, checksum); (4) move the existing `graph.db` and `memories.db` to a quarantine location for forensics; (5) start the daemon with `--bootstrap-from-snapshot <path>`; (6) confirm the daemon emits a `SnapshotBootstrap` event; (7) confirm queries return the expected pre-snapshot state plus post-snapshot replay; (8) document the post-recovery audit checklist. Cross-link to the test `test_replay_from_snapshot_reconstructs_state` in `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/hardening/recovery_tests.rs`.\n   - `## Replay from event log only` — fallback procedure when no snapshot is usable. Steps and post-replay verification.\n   - `## Quarantine and forensics` — procedure for preserving corrupted state for diagnosis without blocking recovery.\n   - `## Corruption recovery` — per-class procedures matching the T82 corruption test scenarios: corrupted payload, mismatched hash, invalid kind, invalid stable reference, truncated file, snapshot version mismatch.\n   - `## Rollback` — how to roll back a bad migration using the inverse migrations from T03's migration policy doc and T84's migration tests.\n   - `## Communicating incidents` — what to log, who to notify, what to capture for the post-incident review.\n4. Cross-link both documents: the runbook's \"Alerts and response\" section links to the recovery playbook for procedures that require recovery; the recovery playbook's intro links back to the runbook for daily-operations context.\n5. Confirm both documents cite spec headings exactly (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Markdown Heading References) — `## Storage Design` for compaction snapshots, `## 3. Event Log` for replay semantics, `## Phase 11: Hardening` for the operational invariants.\n6. Confirm the quoted deploy sequence matches `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy character-for-character.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `docs/operator-guide/2026-05-16-runbook.md` — daily-operations runbook covering deploy, health, alerts, memory hygiene, and backups.\n- `docs/operator-guide/2026-05-16-recovery-playbook.md` — worst-day playbook covering replay-from-snapshot, replay-from-event-log, corruption recovery, rollback, and incident communication.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f docs/operator-guide/2026-05-16-runbook.md` — confirms the runbook exists.\n- `test -f docs/operator-guide/2026-05-16-recovery-playbook.md` — confirms the recovery playbook exists.\n- `grep -q '## Daily operations' docs/operator-guide/2026-05-16-runbook.md` — confirms the daily-operations section exists under its required heading.\n- `grep -q '## Recovery procedures' docs/operator-guide/2026-05-16-recovery-playbook.md` — confirms the recovery-procedures section exists under its required heading.\n- `grep -q '## Replay from snapshot' docs/operator-guide/2026-05-16-recovery-playbook.md` — confirms the replay-from-snapshot procedure exists under its required heading.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, no \"coming soon\" placeholders.\n- Single source of truth: the deploy sequence is quoted from `lattice/CLAUDE.md` exactly once; the runbook references the source rather than fork its own copy.\n- Doc citation: every procedural step that depends on a spec invariant cites the spec heading by exact text; every procedure that depends on a test cites the test name and file (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Markdown Heading References).\n- Operator-friendly: every procedure is numbered, each step is a single action, each action is verifiable.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] Runbook quotes the `pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/` sequence from `lattice/CLAUDE.md` exactly.\n- [ ] Recovery playbook's replay-from-snapshot procedure cross-links to the T82 test that proves it works.\n- [ ] Both